.TH STURY 1 "2026-10" "stury 0.0.1" "General Commands Manual"
.SH NAME
stury \- JSON codec with a jq 1.8.2\-compatible command line
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B stury
[\-cCMnRrsSaejV] [\-\-tab|\-\-indent n] [\-\-arg name value] [\-\-argjson name value] [\-\-slurpfile name file] [\-\-rawfile name file] [\-f file] [\-L dir] [\-\-seq] [\-\-stream] [\-\-ndjson] [\-\-jsonc] [\-\-max\-depth n] [stury options] <jq filter> [file...]
.br
.B stury
.B check
[\-\-ndjson|\-\-seq] [\-\-jsonc] [\-\-strict\-limits] [\-\-max\-depth n] [file...]
.br
.B stury
\-h | \-V | \-\-build\-configuration
.SH DESCRIPTION
stury applies a jq 1.8 filter to JSON inputs and gives jq's results and exit codes. Its messages start with stury: and are worded its own way. It accepts all of jq 1.8's options except \-\-run\-tests, a test\-file runner it does not have. The filter language and those options are described in
.BR jq (1).
The synopsis lists the common ones. This page covers what is specific to stury: how its input handling differs from jq's, and its own options.
.PP
.B stury check
(or
.BR validate )
validates its inputs, accepting any input that
.B stury .
reads without error, and prints nothing when they are valid. It requires at least one JSON text: an empty or whitespace-only input is "No JSON text" and exits 5. It is the subcommand when it is the first argument that is not an option. With no file on a terminal it prints the short usage and exits 2.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-jsonc
Read JSONC (comments and trailing commas), also for \-\-argjson, \-\-jsonargs, \-\-slurpfile (one document per file) and fromjson. Values print with their comments.
.TP
.B \-\-ndjson
Read one JSON text per line.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-depth " n"
Refuse nesting past n levels (default 1024; jq's is 10000).
.TP
.B \-\-strict\-limits
With \fBcheck\fR, keep the library's limits: numbers of at most 256 digits and a scale of at most 4096.
.TP
.B \-V
Print stury\- and the version it was built with: a release version, or the short commit hash from a source build (dev outside a checkout).
.SH INPUT DIFFERENCES FROM JQ
stury reads strict RFC 8259 JSON. It rejects, with exit 5, these inputs that jq accepts: 01, 1., NaN, nan, Infinity, invalid UTF\-8 in a string, a lone low surrogate escape, a NUL after a value, and nesting past \-\-max\-depth. A parse error names the fault and the position of the byte where it was found. In a file after the first, it reads NAME:LINE:COLUMN: FAULT, counted in that file, as check prints it. A trailing comma, a comment or nesting past the cap is followed by the source line, a caret and the flag that would read it. \-\-argjson and \-\-jsonargs keep jq's message and append the fault. Each file ends its texts. The full list is in docs/cli.md, "Differences from jq".
.SH EXIT STATUS
As jq: 1 under \-e when the last output is false or null, 2 for a usage or file error, 3 for a filter that does not compile, 4 under \-e when there is no output, 5 for invalid input or an error on the last input.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR jq (1),
docs/cli.md in stury's source.
